{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BJPMTwippRFVTHV94euFrx","type":"tool_result","content":"{'figure_id': 'protocol_overview', 'required': True, 'purpose': 'Give the reader an immediate picture of the studied perturbation (delete comparisons, refit exactly, test whether an adjacent pair reverses), the removal-search instrument, baselines, controls, and which two designs were measured in the sealed final stage versus the search stage.', 'figure_type': 'diagram', 'preferred_generator': 'generate-concept-figure', 'group_by': 'pipeline_stage', 'metric': 'none (conceptual)', 'split_by': 'none', 'preferred_span': 'full_width', 'target_width_fraction': 1.0, 'layout_rationale': 'ICLR 2026 template is single-column with 5.5 in text width; a left-to-right pipeline with 5 to 6 boxes and short labels needs the full text width to keep font at least 7 pt; place on page 1 or 2.', 'allow_progression': False, 'max_panels': 1, 'max_categories_per_panel': 6, 'max_series_per_panel': 3}\n{'figure_id': 'main_budget_by_case', 'required': True, 'purpose': 'Show the verified minimal removal budget for the four real final cases and the evaluator-owned comparators, making the crowd versus sports contrast, the top versus mid contrast, and the studied-equals-B2 result visible together.', 'figure_type': 'grouped_bar', 'preferred_generator': 'matplotlib', 'group_by': 'case (top pair, mid pair)', 'metric': 'verified removal budget as percent of all comparisons (log scale), k from final.evidence.episodes', 'split_by': 'design (crowd votes, sports games)', 'preferred_span': 'full_width', 'target_width_fraction': 1.0, 'layout_rationale': 'Two side-by-side panels with 2 categories and 3 series each, log axis and a cap line; at 5.5 in total width each panel is about 2.6 in, enough for legible 8 pt labels. Draw studied and B2 bars distinguishable (hatch/offset) since they coincide. Export PDF plus 300 DPI PNG; reference the PDF in LaTeX.', 'allow_progression': False, 'max_panels': 2, 'max_categories_per_panel': 2, 'max_series_per_panel': 3}\n{'figure_id': 'controls_permutation', 'required': True, 'purpose': 'Show that label-permuted tables flip with far fewer deletions than real tables and that the permutations are non-vacuous, so real-data budgets reflect real rating gaps rather than an easy target.', 'figure_type': 'paired_panel_bar', 'preferred_generator': 'matplotlib', 'group_by': 'design (crowd votes, sports games)', 'metric': 'verified removal budget (rows, log scale) real vs permuted; rating-spread ratio permuted/real', 'split_by': 'quantity (budget; spread ratio)', 'preferred_span': 'full_width', 'target_width_fraction': 0.9, 'layout_rationale': 'Two small panels each with 2 categories and 2 series; fits in 0.9 of the 5.5 in text width without shrinking; if it reads poorly it should be converted to a table instead of scaled down.', 'allow_progression': False, 'max_panels': 2, 'max_categories_per_panel': 2, 'max_series_per_panel': 2}\n      \"n_panels\": 2,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"path_pdf\": \"figures/controls_permutation.pdf\",\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/controls_permutation.png\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        2,\n        1\n      ],\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": true,\n      \"uses_progression\": false\n    }\n  ]\n}\n# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.","is_error":fa