{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KoMYWpPfsQz9NoXz6ATvhS","type":"tool_result","content":"39:Public leaderboards built from pairwise comparisons fit a Bradley--Terry model and publish a ranking, yet how many comparisons must change before a ranking reverses is rarely measured. We study the smallest set of del\n49:\\paragraph{What we do.} We measure recorded removal budgets on two held-out tables of real data: a deduplicated Chatbot Arena crowd-vote table and an NBA game-results table. We compare the studied search configuration\n51:\\paragraph{What we find.} (i) Recorded budgets are small in all four held-out cases (4 to 62 of 135,630 crowd rows; 8 to 62 of 12,900 NBA rows), and mid pairs are cheaper than top pairs in both tables. (ii) As a share\n100:Table~\\ref{tab:data} lists the tables. The search procedure receives rows as integer participant indices with a coded outcome; names are not visible to it in the inspected candidate source. The held-out crowd table i\n114:Development & expert judgments & MT-Bench human & 3,355 & 6 models & 68\\\\\n217:No expert-design result exists on held-out data, and the development stage cannot say whether the expert table is easier or harder to reverse than the crowd tables. The expert-judgment design is therefore unmeasured \n222:\\paragraph{What it does not support.} (i) \\emph{No causal claim about evaluation design.} Crowd votes and sports are confounded with table size, participants, era, outcome granularity and tie structure; one table per\n229:We measured how few comparisons must be deleted to reverse the point-estimate order of one adjacent pair in a Bradley--Terry leaderboard, as recorded by the task's evaluator. On two held-out tables of real data, the \n320:Four held-out cases on two designs, two identical episodes and twenty random draws are the entire basis for the held-out claims. The development side adds one expert table of six participants, for which no breakdown","is_erro